<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-05" top="0.5in" left="0.5in" right="0.5in" bottom="0.5in">
  <title>Lecture Worksheet - Tension and Friction</title>


  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            A car enters a banked turn whose radius is
            <m>R</m> (the picture below is seen from behind, the road is curved).
            The road is banked at an angle <m>\theta</m>, and the friction coefficient
            between the tires and the track is <m>\mu</m>.
            There is a maximum and a minimum speed that the car can drive on this turn
            without the tires slipping.
        </p>

        <image source="images\activities\lec5\banked.png">
            <shortdescription></shortdescription>
        </image>
        <p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        At the moment shown, what is the direction of the car's
                        acceleration?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Draw FBDs for when the car is going at its maximum and minimum
                        speeds.
                        Hint for the max speed diagram: think about how the car would
                        skid if it's going too fast.
                        It might be easier to think about a flat turn first.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Which coordinate system seems best suited to solving the
                        problem: horizontal and vertical axes, or parallel and
                        perpendicular to the turn?
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Write Newton's 2nd law for the car when the car is going at the
                        maximum speed.
                        Use <m>f_{s}=\mu N</m> to reduce the number of unknowns.
                        You don't have to solve the equations, but check that you have
                        only 2 unknowns.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
        </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <p>
        <alert>The whirling rope: differential equations and continuous systems.</alert>
        Consider the rope shown in the picture below, which has mass <m>M</m>,
        uniformly distributed through its length <m>L</m>.
        It is pivoted at one end and it spins with constant angular velocity
        <m>\omega</m>.
        Neglecting gravity, how does the tension vary across the length of the rope?
        That is, our goal is to find a function <m>T(r)</m> that gives the tension
        at a distance <m>r</m> from the pivot.
    </p>

    <image source="images\activities\lec5\whirling_rope.png">
        <shortdescription></shortdescription>
    </image>
    <p>
        <ol>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Draw the rope, and put on top of it a radial axis <m>r</m> that
                    measures the distance from the pivot.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Slice up the rope into several pieces of length <m>dr</m> and
                    consider the rope segment at some particular distance <m>r</m>
                    from the origin.
                    That is, the distance from the center of the two ends of the
                    slice are <m>r</m> and <m>r+dr</m>.
                    Draw the FBD for this piece of rope.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Discussion: From your FBD, does the tension increase or decrease
                    as you get further from the pivot?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    How much mass <m>dm</m> does your piece of rope have?
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Write down Newton's second law for this rope segment and us it
                    to find an expression for the change in tension,
                    <m>T(r + dr) - T(r)</m>
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the previous result and take <m>dr\to 0</m> to find
                    <m>\frac{dT}{dr}</m>, the derivative of the tension.
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Discussion: Do we know the tension at any point along the rope?
                    Hint: there's a point at which the rope should be slack
                </p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <p>
                    Using your results from the two previous parts, write a definite
                    integral expression which allows you find the value of
                    <m>T(R)</m> at any point along the rope.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ol>
    </p>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>